description: "Decoherence done properly, the microtubule evidence of 2022–2026, the glymphatic tide that washes the cytoskeleton every night, and an honest account of what physics does and does not permit when a tradition says the self is gathered up again in the next density."
faq:
  - "@type": Question
    name: "Does decoherence disprove quantum consciousness?"
    acceptedAnswer:
      "@type": Answer
      text: "No, and it does not prove it either. Decoherence sets a timescale. Tegmark calculated 10⁻¹³ seconds for a microtubule in 2000; Hagan, Hameroff and Tuszyński recalculated the same formula with corrected parameters in 2002 and got 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁵ seconds. Orch OR needs about 25 milliseconds. Even the friendly figure is a hundredfold short, and that gap is the whole argument."
  - "@type": Question
    name: "Is there experimental evidence for quantum effects in microtubules?"
    acceptedAnswer:
      "@type": Answer
      text: "There is real experimental evidence for quantum optical effects, and no evidence yet for quantum computation. Babcock and colleagues demonstrated ultraviolet superradiance in tryptophan networks at room temperature in 2024. Kalra and Tuszyński measured exciton propagation in microtubules that anaesthetics damp. Khan and colleagues found that the microtubule stabiliser epothilone B delays anaesthetic unconsciousness in rats with a large effect size. Each result is genuine; none of them shows a microtubule holding a computational state for the tens of milliseconds a theory of consciousness would require."
  - "@type": Question
    name: "What has the lymphatic system to do with microtubules?"
    acceptedAnswer:
      "@type": Answer
      text: "Three real connections, none of them mystical. The brain's glymphatic and meningeal lymphatic route clears tau, which is a microtubule-associated protein shed from the lattice. The cerebrospinal fluid is swept by ependymal cilia, which are microtubule axonemes, so the cytoskeleton physically drives the tide. And anaesthesia alters both microtubule quantum optics and fluid clearance at once. The housekeeping and the candidate substrate are the same tissue on the same nightly cycle."
  - "@type": Question
    name: "Does physics allow a self to be remembered after death?"
    acceptedAnswer:
      "@type": Answer
      text: "Physics forbids the destruction of quantum information, not the scrambling of it. Unitarity says the information that constituted you is still in the universe; it says nothing about whether it remains in a form anyone could read, and every known process scrambles it rapidly. So the traditional claim that the self is gathered up again is not ruled out by physics and is not evidenced by it. That is the honest position and we hold it without flinching in either direction."
