    <p class="plate-note">The commonest error in popular writing is to say that decoherence collapses the
    wavefunction. It does not. Nothing is destroyed and nothing chooses. What happens is that the system becomes
    <em>entangled</em> with its surroundings &mdash; one stray photon, one air molecule, one phonon in the
    lattice &mdash; and the delicate phase relation that made interference possible is no longer a property of
    the system alone. It is now spread across the system <em>and</em> everything it touched, which is
    effectively everywhere, and we have no way to gather it back. The interference does not vanish from the
    universe. It vanishes from our reach.</p>
