<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <h2>I &middot; Decoherence, Done Properly</h2>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Formally: write the state of a system as a density matrix. Superposition
  lives in the <em>off-diagonal</em> terms, the ones that encode phase relationships between possibilities.
  Couple the system to an environment with many degrees of freedom and those off-diagonal terms decay
  exponentially, at a rate set by how strongly and how often the environment is interacting. The diagonal terms
  &mdash; the plain probabilities &mdash; survive untouched. The maths was developed by
  <strong>H. Dieter Zeh</strong> from 1970, given its decisive form by <strong>Erich Joos and Zeh</strong> in
  1985, and turned into the modern account of why the world looks classical by <strong>Wojciech Zurek</strong>,
  whose <em>einselection</em> explains why position, and not some exotic superposition of positions, is the
  property the environment keeps measuring.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The timescales are savage. A dust grain of a hundredth of a millimetre,
  in air, decoheres in something like 10<sup>&minus;31</sup> seconds; in intergalactic vacuum, struck only by
  the cosmic microwave background, it still goes in about a second. A large molecule can be held coherent in a
  vacuum chamber &mdash; the Arndt group in Vienna have run interference with molecules of over 25,000 atomic
  mass units &mdash; but it has to be cold, isolated and in the dark. This is why the physicist's instinct
  about a warm, wet, electrically noisy brain is not snobbery. It is arithmetic.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> And here is the part almost every popular account skips, which this order
  insists on: <strong>decoherence does not solve the measurement problem</strong>. It explains beautifully why
  you never see a cat in superposition. It does not explain why <em>this</em> outcome happened rather than that
  one. Zurek says so; Joos says so. Many-worlds takes the surviving branches to be real; objective-collapse
  theories such as GRW and Penrose's gravitational scheme add a genuine physical reduction; the pilot-wave
  picture never had the problem. Nothing in the last fifty years has closed it. Anyone who tells you quantum
  mechanics is settled at the bottom has not read the bottom.</p>

  <p><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Hold on to one consequence, because the rest of this page leans on it.
  Decoherence is not a wall. It is a <em>clock</em>. The question is never &ldquo;can quantum effects happen in
  biology&rdquo; &mdash; they demonstrably do, in photosynthesis, in enzyme tunnelling, in the radical-pair
  compass a robin steers by. The question is always: <em>for how long, over what distance, and is that long
  enough to matter for the job claimed?</em></p>
</section>

