    <p class="plate-note">Every claim in this field reduces to a position on this line. Tegmark's 2000 estimate
    and the Hagan&ndash;Hameroff&ndash;Tuszy&#324;ski recalculation of 2002 use the same formula with different
    assumptions about screening, separation and ordered water, and land nine orders of magnitude apart. The
    measured tryptophan fluorescence lifetimes are nanoseconds; delayed luminescence from microtubules reaches
    hundreds of milliseconds but is a far weaker signal. Orch OR needs about 25 milliseconds, the period of a
    40 Hz gamma cycle. The friendliest serious calculation is still roughly a hundredfold short of the
    requirement.</p>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <h2>II &middot; The Microtubule, and What Has Actually Been Measured</h2>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> First the object itself, which is remarkable before anyone mentions
  consciousness. A microtubule is a hollow tube about 25 nanometres across, built from &alpha;&beta;-tubulin
  dimers 8 nanometres long, arranged in &mdash; almost always &mdash; <strong>thirteen protofilaments</strong>.
  It is the cell's skeleton, its railway and its ruler: it builds the mitotic spindle, carries cargo along
  kinesin and dynein tracks, holds the axon open, and forms the 9+2 axoneme of every cilium and flagellum you
  possess. Tubulin is among the most conserved proteins in eukaryotic life. Taxol, colchicine and vinblastine
  all work by interfering with it, which is why so much of chemotherapy is, at bottom, cytoskeletal sabotage.</p>

  <p><span class="hist">&#9670;</span> <strong>Orch OR</strong> &mdash; Orchestrated Objective Reduction,
  Penrose and Hameroff from 1994 &mdash; makes three separate claims, and the commonest mistake is to accept or
  reject them as a bundle. One: tubulin can hold quantum states. Two: those states are orchestrated by the
  surrounding neurobiology and persist long enough to compute. Three: they end in Penrose's gravitational
  objective reduction, a genuine physical collapse whose timing is set by E = &#8463;/t, and each such event is
  a moment of proto-experience. The first claim now has real support. The second is unproven. The third is a
  conjecture in quantum gravity with no experimental purchase at all.</p>

  <h3>The evidence, 2022&ndash;2026</h3>
  <ul class="biblio">
    <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Anaesthetics damp microtubule excitons.</strong> Kalra,
    Scholes, Tuszy&#324;ski and colleagues (2023) showed ultraviolet-induced excitation propagating further and
    persisting longer in microtubules than classical diffusion predicts, and that isoflurane and etomidate both
    shorten it. Real, published, and directly relevant.</li>
    <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Superradiance at room temperature.</strong> Babcock, Kurian
    and colleagues (<em>J. Phys. Chem. B</em>, 2024) predicted and then measured ultraviolet superradiance in
    mega-networks of tryptophan &mdash; tens of thousands of aromatic residues acting cooperatively, with the
    effect <em>strengthening</em> as the structure gets bigger. That is the opposite of what the warm-and-wet
    objection expects, and it is the single most important result in the field this decade.</li>
    <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Stabilise the lattice, resist the anaesthetic.</strong> Khan,
    Wiest and colleagues (<em>eNeuro</em>, 2024) gave rats the microtubule stabiliser epothilone B and found
    they took over a minute longer to lose the righting reflex under isoflurane, with a very large effect
    (Cohen's <em>d</em> &asymp; 1.9). Broadly replicated in mice in 2025.</li>
    <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>And now the brake.</strong> Tuszy&#324;ski, who ran the
    experiments, reported the measured fluorescence lifetimes as 2&ndash;5 nanoseconds over a correlated region
    of about 5 nanometres &mdash; roughly the size of one tubulin dimer &mdash; and said in plain words that
    this is <em>&ldquo;quite long, but falls short of physiological relevance&rdquo;</em>. That sentence, from a
    principal investigator sympathetic to the theory, is the most honest line in the literature and it is almost
    never quoted by advocates.</li>
    <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>The logical gap in the anaesthesia argument.</strong> &ldquo;We
    cannot think of a classical way binding to microtubules would cause unconsciousness, therefore quantum&rdquo;
    is an argument from absence. Microtubules have entirely classical jobs &mdash; trafficking, receptor
    delivery, axonal transport, the physical maintenance of the synapse &mdash; any of which, disturbed, could
    plausibly dim a brain. The epothilone result is a genuine finding about microtubules and a weak finding
    about quantum mechanics.</li>
    <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>And the reviews are partisan, in both directions.</strong>
    Wiest's 2025 paper in <em>Neuroscience of Consciousness</em> is an advocate's brief, and should be read as
    one; the 2026 <em>Frontiers in Psychology</em> critical review is the more careful survey and finds the
    programme physically unproven but, for the first time, genuinely testable. Kerskens and L&oacute;pez-P&eacute;rez's
    2022 MRI signal, read by some as macroscopic brain entanglement, has a serious methodological critique
    against it and should not be leaned on.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>Where this order stands.</strong> Quantum optical effects in
  microtubules are real and measured. Quantum <em>computation</em> in microtubules is not shown. Orch OR has
  moved, in twenty years, from &ldquo;physically impossible&rdquo; to &ldquo;physically unproven and
  experimentally live&rdquo;, which is a substantial journey. We would bet against
  it and we would fund it, and we see no contradiction in that.</p>
</section>

