<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <h2>III &middot; The Lymphatic Brain, and Why It Belongs on This Page</h2>

  <p><span class="hist">&#9670;</span> For a century every medical textbook said the central nervous system had
  no lymphatics. In 2015 two groups &mdash; <strong>Louveau and Kipnis</strong> in Virginia, and
  <strong>Aspelund and Alitalo</strong> in Helsinki &mdash; independently found functional lymphatic vessels
  running in the dural sinuses, draining to the deep cervical nodes. It was a genuine rediscovery: the Italian
  anatomist <strong>Paolo Mascagni</strong> had drawn meningeal lymphatics in 1787 and been disbelieved for two
  hundred and twenty-eight years.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Three years earlier <strong>Jeffrey Iliff and Maiken Nedergaard</strong>
  had described the <em>glymphatic</em> system (2012) &mdash; glial-dependent, lymphatic-like &mdash; a
  perivascular route by which cerebrospinal fluid is pushed into the brain along arteries, exchanges with
  interstitial fluid through <strong>aquaporin-4</strong> water channels on astrocyte endfeet, and carries
  solutes out along veins. In 2013 <strong>Xie</strong> and colleagues reported in <em>Science</em> that the
  interstitial space widens by roughly 60 per cent in sleep and that clearance of amyloid-&beta; roughly
  doubles. It became one of the most cited ideas in neuroscience within a decade.</p>

  <h3>And then the field had a proper argument, which we are going to report</h3>
  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> In May 2024 <strong>Miao, Franks and colleagues</strong> published in
  <em>Nature Neuroscience</em> a study using a quite different method &mdash; dye injected into tissue rather
  than into the CSF, with photobleaching to measure diffusion &mdash; and found clearance <em>reduced</em> by
  about 30 per cent in sleep and 50 per cent under anaesthesia: the exact opposite of the orthodoxy. Nedergaard
  called the work badly done and filed a formal <em>Matters Arising</em>, published in March 2025. The 2025
  SLEEP meeting in Seattle staged a live debate on it.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The weight of evidence has since swung back, with better mechanism
  attached. <strong>Jiang-Xie and colleagues</strong> (<em>Nature</em>, 2024) showed that synchronised neuronal
  activity itself drives CSF perfusion; a companion paper found that 40 Hz gamma sensory stimulation promotes
  glymphatic clearance of amyloid. The Nedergaard group's 2025 <em>Cell</em> paper identified the pump:
  <strong>noradrenaline oscillations from the locus coeruleus during NREM sleep drive slow vasomotion</strong>,
  and the arteries' slow squeezing is what moves the fluid &mdash; with the uncomfortable corollary that
  zolpidem suppresses those oscillations and reduces clearance. In 2026 a randomised crossover trial in 39
  people (<em>Nature Communications</em>) found that normal sleep raised morning plasma amyloid-&beta; and tau
  relative to sleep deprivation, which is what brain-to-blood clearance looks like from the outside, and
  scaled with NREM duration.</p>

  <p><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> We report the quarrel rather than the conclusion because the quarrel is
  the science. A result that contradicted a decade of orthodoxy was published in a top journal, attacked on
  method, formally rebutted, debated in public, and partly absorbed. That is the apparatus working. A page that
  gave you only the tidy version would be lying by omission.</p>

  <h3>The three joins between the tide and the lattice</h3>
  <p class="muted">Here is why this is on the same page as microtubules, and none of the three requires any
  mysticism at all.</p>
  <ul class="biblio">
    <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>What the tide carries away is a microtubule protein.</strong>
    Tau is a <em>microtubule-associated protein</em>: its day job is binding and stabilising the lattice. In
    disease it becomes hyperphosphorylated, lets go of the microtubule, aggregates, and is then precisely what
    the glymphatic route must clear. The brain's nightly housekeeping exists in large part to carry off the
    debris of its own cytoskeleton.</li>
    <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>The tide is swept by microtubules.</strong> Cerebrospinal
    fluid is moved along the ventricles by the beating of <strong>ependymal cilia</strong>, and a cilium is a
    9+2 axoneme &mdash; nine doublet microtubules around two singlets, driven by dynein. The cytoskeleton is not
    a metaphorical participant in the flow. It is the oar.</li>
    <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>One switch moves both.</strong> Anaesthesia dampens
    microtubule quantum optics <em>and</em> changes fluid clearance. Sleep stage, noradrenaline, vasomotion,
    consciousness and clearance are all tied to the same slow oscillations. We are not claiming a causal chain
    here &mdash; we are pointing out that the candidate substrate of experience and the maintenance system that
    keeps it clean run on one timetable, in one tissue, under one set of controls. That is a reason to study
    them together. It is not a reason to say the lymph is conscious, and we will not say it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> One more honest line, because the wellness internet has got hold of this
  one. &ldquo;Lymphatic drainage&rdquo; massage, dry brushing, rebounders and detox teas have nothing to do with
  the glymphatic system, which is inside your skull and driven by your arteries and your sleep. The
  interventions with actual evidence behind them are dull: enough slow-wave sleep, aerobic exercise, probably
  sleeping on your side, and not sedating yourself with the drugs that flatten noradrenaline oscillation.</p>
</section>

<div class="divider">&#10022;</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <h2>IV &middot; What Is Conserved, and What Is Merely Conserved</h2>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Quantum mechanics is <em>unitary</em>: evolution preserves information.
  Two distinct states never become one state. The <strong>no-hiding theorem</strong> (Braunstein and Pati,
  2007) makes it sharper &mdash; if information disappears from a system it must be present somewhere in the
  rest of the universe, and cannot be concealed in the correlations alone. Hawking conceded the black-hole
  information paradox in 2004 on essentially this basis. In the strict physical sense, <strong>nothing that has
  ever existed has been deleted</strong>.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Now the cold part, and it must be said in the same breath. Conserved is
  not the same as recoverable. Burn a book and the information is, in principle, still in the smoke, the heat
  and the light; in practice it is <em>scrambled</em> &mdash; spread across so many degrees of freedom and so
  many correlations that no physically realisable measurement could reassemble a sentence. Decoherence is
  exactly this process of scrambling, and it runs in microseconds. A brain at 37 &deg;C is the most efficient
  scrambler in the room.</p>

  <p><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The honest framing is therefore three-way, and only one of the three is
  actually forbidden.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <figure id="fig-5" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate V</span> What the Physics Actually
    Forbids</figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 660 330" role="img" aria-label="Three rows: information conserved but scrambled, information conserved and retrievable, and information destroyed. Physics forbids only the third">
