<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <dl class="glossary">
        <dt>Decoherence</dt>
        <dd>The loss of interference through entanglement with an environment. Explains classical appearance; does not select an outcome.</dd>
        <dt>Density matrix</dt>
        <dd>The full description of a quantum state, in which superposition lives in the off-diagonal terms.</dd>
        <dt>Einselection</dt>
        <dd>Zurek's environment-induced superselection: the environment repeatedly measures certain properties, so those are the ones that appear classical.</dd>
        <dt>Unitarity</dt>
        <dd>The rule that quantum evolution preserves information. Distinct states stay distinct.</dd>
        <dt>No-hiding theorem</dt>
        <dd>Braunstein and Pati, 2007: information lost from a system must be fully present elsewhere, not merely in correlations.</dd>
        <dt>Objective reduction</dt>
        <dd>Penrose's proposal that superposition of spacetime geometry collapses physically, after a time set by E = &#8463;/t.</dd>
        <dt>Orch OR</dt>
        <dd>Orchestrated objective reduction: the Penrose&ndash;Hameroff claim that such collapses, orchestrated in microtubules, are moments of experience.</dd>
        <dt>Microtubule</dt>
        <dd>A 25 nm hollow tube of thirteen protofilaments of &alpha;&beta;-tubulin. Cell skeleton, railway, spindle and the core of every cilium.</dd>
        <dt>Tryptophan network</dt>
        <dd>The dense lattice of aromatic residues inside tubulin that carries the measured optical effects &mdash; and where anaesthetics bind.</dd>
        <dt>Superradiance</dt>
        <dd>Dicke, 1954: emitters close enough together radiate cooperatively, faster and brighter than they would alone. Measured in tryptophan mega-networks in 2024.</dd>
        <dt>Glymphatic system</dt>
        <dd>The perivascular, glia-dependent route by which CSF exchanges with interstitial fluid and carries solutes out of the brain.</dd>
        <dt>Aquaporin-4</dt>
        <dd>The water channel on astrocyte endfeet through which that exchange happens. Its polarisation declines with age.</dd>
        <dt>Meningeal lymphatics</dt>
        <dd>Lymphatic vessels in the dura draining to the deep cervical nodes. Drawn in 1787, rediscovered in 2015.</dd>
        <dt>Tau</dt>
        <dd>A microtubule-associated protein. Stabilises the lattice; when it detaches and aggregates it becomes the waste the tide must carry.</dd>
        <dt>Ependymal cilia</dt>
        <dd>The 9+2 microtubule axonemes lining the ventricles, whose beating moves cerebrospinal fluid.</dd>
        <dt>Vasomotion</dt>
        <dd>Slow rhythmic constriction and dilation of arteries. Driven by noradrenaline in NREM sleep, and the glymphatic pump.</dd>
        <dt>Density (Law of One)</dt>
        <dd>One of eight graded states of consciousness in the Ra material, forming an octave in which the eighth is the first of the next.</dd>
        <dt>Time/space</dt>
        <dd>In the same material, the metaphysical counterpart of space/time, where the review between incarnations is said to occur.</dd>
        <dt>Terminal lucidity</dt>
        <dd>The documented, unexplained return of clarity in advanced dementia shortly before death.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Questions We Cannot Answer</h2>
      <p class="muted">The edge of this page, named rather than painted over.</p>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Why does one outcome occur rather than another? Decoherence does not say, and nothing else has.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Can any measurable quantum coherence in a microtubule be shown to last 25 milliseconds in a living brain?</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Does sleep increase clearance, or does it merely change what the tracer tells us? The 2024 result is not yet fully absorbed.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Is there a physical difference between &ldquo;information is conserved&rdquo; and &ldquo;information is remembered&rdquo;, and could any experiment find it?</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Why does a brain with no measurable cortical output produce structured, reportable experience at all, as AWARE II suggests it sometimes does?</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> What would it even look like to test the claim that a life is reviewed from inside every position it occupied?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small">Six doors. We would rather hold them open with a chair than pretend to have keys.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

